<h2>Packaging the Wiki Engine as a Plugin — wiki-plugin and Sync Discipline</h2>
<p>This knowledge system is not exclusive to this site. The structure, the pipeline, and the validation gates are packaged as a <strong>Claude Code plugin (wiki-plugin)</strong>, so that <strong>any repository can bootstrap the same knowledge system</strong>. This document explains what was made into a plugin and why, and what discipline is in place so that the original and the copy never drift apart.</p>

<h3>Why — Separating Engine from Content</h3>
<p>This wiki is made of two layers: the <strong>engine</strong> (the four-level structure, the registration pipeline, the index builder, the validation gates, the viewer) and the <strong>content</strong> (the documents, the curated concepts, the cluster names). Every principle the previous four parts described is a property of the engine, so it is <strong>reusable</strong> — install this engine when you start knowledge management for a new project, and the same knowledge graph grows from the moment you drop in a document. Content, by contrast (this site's <code>doc-entries</code> and cluster labels), is a site-specific asset and is therefore <strong>excluded from the bundle</strong>.</p>

<h3>Composition — Four Skills plus a Tool Snapshot</h3>
<p>The plugin installs from the marketplace in two lines (<code>/plugin marketplace add</code> → <code>/plugin install</code>). Once installed, the four phases of running a wiki arrive as <strong>skills</strong>:</p>
<div class="tbl-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Skill</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>wiki-init</code></td><td>Bootstraps a new wiki project — structure, nav tree, index pipeline, validation gates, and CI, all at once.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>add-doc</code></td><td>Enforces the whole workflow for adding a document — file → tree registration → curation → index → Work Log → validation.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>index</code></td><td>Regenerates the knowledge index, document dates, and the AI consumption layer, in compliance with the schema contract.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>validate</code></td><td>Runs the quality validators in one batch and interprets ERROR/WARN — down to "what is a defect and what is a review signal."</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Along with the skills, a <strong>snapshot of the operational tools</strong> — the index builder, the validators, and so on — is enclosed. If the project has its own <code>tools/</code>, that one takes precedence (the dogfooding path); if not, the copy bundled with the plugin is used. The SPA viewer (the screens) needs no separate bundle: because adding the marketplace clones the entire repository, the latest real thing is always present locally.</p>

<h3>Discipline — a Snapshot Must Match the Original</h3>
