<p>The weakness of the snapshot approach is <strong>drift</strong> (fixing only the original and forgetting the copy). So a dedicated gate <strong>compares the original <code>tools/</code> with the bundle byte for byte</strong> and fails CI if they differ. On top of that, a PR that changes the bundle passes only if it comes with a <strong>plugin version bump</strong> — a release discipline that lets installers track "when what changed" by version. The gate itself and the site-specific data (cluster labels, curation entries) are on the bundle exclusion list.</p>

<h3>Dogfooding — the First User Is This Wiki Itself</h3>
<p>This wiki is wiki-plugin's <strong>first user and reference implementation</strong>. The daily operation of this site — adding documents, regenerating the index, validating — is itself the plugin's integration test, and gaps found in operation (a missing guide, a hole in a gate) feed back as plugin improvements. Every principle from the previous four parts — <a href="#!kgs-overview">how knowledge accumulates</a>, <a href="#!kgs-edges">the rules that connect it</a>, <a href="#!kgs-views">the views that read it</a>, <a href="#!kgs-ai-layer">AI consumption</a> — lives inside this plugin, so this series doubles as a design commentary on wiki-plugin.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Installation</span>
